    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The city was a Greek colony called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> for nearly a thousand years before Constantine refounded it as a new capital in 330, and it was named Constantinople in his honour.</li><li>It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> in the east and then of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> until the Ottoman conquest of 29 May 1453, and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> until the sultanate was abolished in 1922.</li><li>Its triple land walls kept out attackers for nearly nine hundred years, yet the city fell to a Christian army in 1204 and to an Ottoman army in 1453.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> comes from the Greek phrase eis tin Polin, 'to the city', which local people had long used; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> began to urge other countries to use it after 1930.</p></aside>
    </section>

