    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 657 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Greek colonists from Megara settle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, by the date most often given</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">196</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> razes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> for backing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pescennius-niger">Pescennius Niger</a>, then rebuilds it and briefly renames it Augusta Antonina</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">330</span><span class="rf-text">Constantine consecrates the refounded city as the new capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, first called Nova Roma</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">413</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> builds the triple land walls, finished in 414</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">532</span><span class="rf-text">The Nika riots burn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>; Justinian's new church is dedicated in 537</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1204</span><span class="rf-text">The Fourth Crusade takes and plunders the city, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a> holds it until 1261</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1453</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> takes Constantinople after a siege that began on 6 April</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1930</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>'s government urges other countries to use the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/byzas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/byzas.jpg" alt="Byzas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/byzas">Byzas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coinage_with_Byzas_2nd_3rd_century_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">World Imaging, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> stood on a peninsula at the south-eastern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horn">Golden Horn</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a>, across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a> from the town of Chalcedon. The site lay on the land route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-roman-province">Asia</a> and on the sea route from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and the Golden Horn gave it a spacious harbour. People lived in the area from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>: a settlement dated to 6700 BCE was found in 2008 during work on the Yenikapı station and the Marmaray tunnel, and on the Asian side the Fikirtepe mound has artefacts from 5500 to 3500 BCE.</p><p>The city was founded around 657 BCE, according to one account, by colonists from the Greek city of Megara. Another account gives 667 BCE for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantion">Byzantion</a> and 685 BCE for the settlement at Chalcedon, and the two do not agree. Greek legend names a king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzas">Byzas</a> as the colonists' leader and as the founder who gave the city its name. Hesychius of Miletus reported that some claimed Megarians sailed there under Byzas and invented the story that his name was attached to the city, and he gave other versions as well. The name is also thought to be Thracian, perhaps from a personal name Byzas. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> named an earlier settlement, Lygos, possibly Thracian, whose few surviving walls lie near Seraglio Point.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantine-i.jpg" alt="Constantine I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statua_di_Costantino_ai_musei_capitolini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Merulana, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> was never a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, Corinth or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, but it had steady growth as a trading city. In 324 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> refounded it on the site of the old town, at first as Nova Roma, and it was consecrated on 11 May 330. He divided it into 14 regions, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was divided. The building was done in great haste, and columns, marbles, doors and tiles were taken from temples across the empire. On 18 May 332 he announced free distributions of food, said at the time to have been 80,000 rations a day from 117 points. The Hippodrome seated over 80,000 people, and the Mese, a colonnaded street, ran from the Augustaion to the Golden Gate.</p><p>In 413 and 414 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> built the 18-metre triple land walls, and on 27 February 425 he founded a university. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> the population reached about 500,000, though some scholars suggest it could have been higher. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a> was dedicated on 26 December 537 and is said to have cost 20,000 pounds of gold. From the mid-5th century to the early 13th century the city was the largest and wealthiest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and in the 9th and 10th centuries its population was between 500,000 and 800,000. The Ottoman city grew from about 80,000 at the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> to 300,000 by the 18th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii.jpg" alt="Mehmed II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bellini,_Gentile_-_Sultan_Mehmet_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gentile Bellini, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> in 512 BCE, because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a> was narrowest there and suited a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontoon-bridge">pontoon bridge</a>. A Greek army under the Spartan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a> took it in 478 BCE, and it was subordinate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and, after 411 BCE, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. A treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> of about 150 BCE let it enter Roman rule unharmed. After it sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pescennius-niger">Pescennius Niger</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> besieged and razed it in 196, then rebuilt it, and for a time it was called Augusta Antonina. The name was soon dropped.</p><p>Constantine proclaimed the city the capital in 330, and it became the sole capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> once the west was lost in the 5th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komnenian-dynasty">Komnenian dynasty</a> ruled from 1081 to 1185. After the sack of 1204 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a> held the city, with Baldwin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> chosen as its first emperor, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> recaptured it in 1261. The Palaiologan emperors moved their residence to the Blachernae palace. By the early 15th century the empire was reduced to Constantinople and its surroundings and the despotate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> made it the fourth and final Ottoman capital in 1453, and it remained one until the sultanate was abolished in 1922. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a> was proclaimed on 29 October 1923 and the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Constantinople&#8221; cites where it covers 1261–1453: Palaiologan Era and the Fall of Constantinople:</p><ul><li>Roger Crowley, Constantinople (Faber &amp; Faber, 2013)</li><li>David Nicolle, Constantinople 1453: The end of Byzantium (Praeger, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basil-ii.jpg" alt="Basil II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilios_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">from the Middle Ages, unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city's by-names included Basileuousa, the Queen of Cities, and in everyday Greek it was simply the City. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> it was a Greek-speaking city and the seat of the Ecumenical Patriarch. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-prefect">Book of the Eparch</a>, of the 10th century, shows tradesmen organised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> supervised by the Eparch, who regulated production, prices, imports and exports; each guild had a monopoly and no one could belong to two. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">Chariot</a> racing in the Hippodrome gave the Blues and the Greens a political voice, and the people there acclaimed new emperors and criticised ministers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> claimed that more than 500 prostitutes worked along the Mese, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, who travelled there in 1325, wrote that most of the artisans and sellers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a> were women.</p><p>In the 12th century Venetians had trading quarters on the north side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horn">Golden Horn</a>. Toward the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a>'s reign about 60,000 to 80,000 foreigners lived in a city of about 400,000, and in 1171 there was a Jewish community of 2,500. In 980 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> received 6,000 Varangian warriors from Prince Vladimir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> as a bodyguard.</p><p>After 1453 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> brought Muslims, Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in by force and by invitation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1477 counted 9,486 houses of Muslims, 3,743 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and 1,647 of Jews. The Grand Bazaar dates from 1455 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a> from 1459. Mehmed re-established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> patriarchate, and the architect Sinan designed many mosques under Suleiman I.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Constantinople&#8221; cites where it covers Culture, Architecture and coinage:</p><ul><li>George P. Majeska, Russian Travelers to Constantinople in the Fourteenth and Fifteenth Centuries (Dumbarton Oaks, 1984)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/greek-fire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/greek-fire.jpg" alt="Greek fire" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/greek-fire">Greek fire</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greekfire-madridskylitzes1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nika-riots">Nika riots</a> of 532 began in the Hippodrome among the Blues and Greens. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> over 30,000 people were involved. The fires set by the mobs burned the Theodosian church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>, which Justinian replaced. The Plague of Justinian struck in 541 and 542 and killed, by one estimate, 20 to 40 per cent of the inhabitants, though revisionist historians suggest fewer. The Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> besieged the city in 674 to 678 and 717 to 718, and the walls held while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-fire">Greek fire</a> destroyed their fleets.</p><p>In 1203 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> was diverted against Constantinople and breached the sea walls on 27 July. After a second rising in February 1204 an attack failed on 6 April and another from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horn">Golden Horn</a> succeeded on 12 April, and the city was given over to pillage for three days. Steven Runciman called the sack unparalleled in history and described murder, rape and the destruction of churches and libraries. Alice-Mary Talbot cites an estimated population of 400,000, about a third of whom were homeless afterwards.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII</a> took the city in 1261 it had 35,000 people, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> reached it in 1347. In 1453 it held about 50,000 by one count, and 30,000 to 36,000 by another. George Sphrantzes counted 4,773 Greek defenders, 200 foreigners and about 3,000 volunteers for a perimeter of twelve miles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> besieged the city from 6 April, using a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> cast by the Hungarian Urban, and it fell on 29 May 1453. Soldiers were given three days to plunder, and the number of people taken captive is given as about 33,000 or 45,000.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia.jpg" alt="Hagia Sophia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hagia_Sophia_(228968325).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adli Wahid, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a> was made a mosque after the conquest of 1453, and the Church of the Holy Apostles was pulled down to make room for the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>. Byzantine gold coinage, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidus">solidus</a>, became the bezant used across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. When the city fell, thousands of Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> were taken by refugees to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where they helped stimulate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. The city's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, who came to be called the Ecumenical Patriarch, made it a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and this contributed to the differences that led to the Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-west-schism">Schism of 1054</a>. In Islamic belief the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-constantinople">conquest of Constantinople</a> is one of the signs of the end time.</p><p>The name changed slowly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> comes from the Greek eis tin Polin and is first attested in Armenian and Arabic from the 10th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> used it and also Kostantiniyye, and Western languages mostly kept Constantinople until the early 20th century. After the Latin alphabet was adopted in 1928 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> urged other countries to use Istanbul, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> State Department did so from May 1930. The population fell from an estimated 1,125,000 in 1914 to about 500,000 in 1924, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> left, a departure hastened by the Istanbul pogrom of September 1955. By 2000 the metropolitan population exceeded 10 million.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/akathist">Akathist Hymn<span>c. 500</span></a><a href="/songs/cherubikon">Cherubikon (the Cherubic Hymn)<span>c. 570</span></a><a href="/songs/hymn-of-kassiani">Hymn of Kassiani<span>c. 843</span></a><a href="/songs/lhomme-arme">L'homme armé (The Armed Man)<span>c. 1453</span></a><a href="/songs/the-red-army-is-the-strongest">The Red Army Is the Strongest<span>1920</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Founded as the Greek colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> around 657 BCE, the city was refounded by Constantine in 330 and was capital of the Roman, Byzantine and Ottoman empires. Besieged many times, it was sacked in 1204 and taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1453, and is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>.</em></p>

